A||||6||fierosound||Spring rates on different models of Fiero?||||5||
Z||000000||fierosound||04-30-2000||12:19 PM||||Anyone have sping rates for the different models of Fiero?<P>I've got an 87 GT and would like to change the springs.  It rides very stiff now with the stock GT springs.  I would like to lower the car without making the suspension stiffer than it is. <P>Cutting the stock spings will lower the car, but stiffen them up as well.  Aftermarket lowering springs are advertised as being stiffer than stock.  <P>Using coupe or SE springs (which I believe have a softer spring rate) should soften the ride.  Cutting them would lower the car and stiffen them to perhaps no more than stock GT levels.<P>I'm just looking for the spring rates to see what the options are.  Anyone have these??||||reg||5
Z||000001||GT Bastard||04-30-2000||03:42 PM||||That sounds like an interesting idea... Just make sure you're not getting the springs from a WS6 Fiero.  Sorry I don't have the springrates.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||04-30-2000||09:53 PM||||You'll need a base coupe. Even then you'll need to run the RPO list to make sure it wasn't ordered with the beafed suspension.<P>You'll find SE's and SC's likely all have the same springs as the GT.<P>I don't have all my notes handy..... <P>There is a 6xx 7xx 8yy 9yy code set.<P>you'll see a 6 and 7 code with the same letters/numbers for xx. you see an 8 and 9 with the same arangement. Those are the actual spring codes. They only seem to show up on cars with suspension options. (Mine are 6HZ 7HZ and 8WY 9WY.) Nope, I don't know what the rates are by code. I do know that set is real stiff. even with crappy struts in the thing.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||05-01-2000||12:37 AM||||I don't know how acurate these are but from what I remember the stock GT spring rates are 275lbs per inch. (I think coupe springs are 230 and 85 GT/SE/Formula are 260) The Eibach springs are 325lbs per inch. I seem to remember these numbers were E-mailed to me from a fiero website that had a message board. (the site is no longer running) So I cannot vouch for their authenticity. I have an 86 SE and I put in the Eibach's. Which lowered the car 1". I have exactly 5" from ground to the bottom of the aero skirt. On a friends GT he took the stock springs and cut 1 coil off. This caused the car to lower 1/4", ride was the same as stock. We are going to install a set of Eibachs on it within a few weeks. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierosound||05-09-2000||12:23 AM||||Thanks for the info guys.  I've got a spring manufacturer near me, so I'll see if they can let me "browse" the books to get this info.  They may even  wind the springs as per my needs... I have to ask...<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ed||05-09-2000||12:35 AM||||The springs are stiffer because of the heavier V-6 engine.  <P>I say that my 4 cylinder was the most comfortable car I've owned.  The V-6 is the second.  <P>Be careful, the stiffer springs were put into the 6 for a reason.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||TOM||05-09-2000||12:43 AM||||cutting the springs does not make them stiffer it just makes them shorter. A lowering spring is stiffer or progressive. So it wont hit the stops.||||reg||1
